# Cline Rules — Ant Nuptial Flight Predictor

Project context and guardrails for Cline (AI coding agent) sessions.

## What this is
A Flutter app (Android / iOS / Web) that downloads weather at the user's
location and predicts the likelihood that queen ants are on a nuptial
flight today and for the next week. Also has an Android home-screen
widget and background-fetch notifications.

Repo: https://github.com/bradrushworth/nuptialflight
Web: https://nuptialflight.app/

## Build / tooling notes (IMPORTANT — read before running commands)
- Flutter SDK lives at `C:/Users/Brad/flutter` (NOT the pub cache).
- The agent's *primary working directory* is often a pub-cache `android`
  folder. ALWAYS target the real project
  `C:/Users/Brad/StudioProjects/nuptialflight` for git/flutter.
- `git` must be invoked as
  `git -C "C:/Users/Brad/StudioProjects/nuptialflight" ...`
  because the shell CWD is not the project.
- `flutter analyze` on the whole project takes ~2 min the first time
  (resolves packages). Run it from the project ROOT, not a sub-path,
  otherwise it resolves paths against the wrong CWD and errors with
  "You provided the path '...' however it does not exist on disk".
- Requires `assets/.env` with `OPENWEATHERMAP_API_KEY=<key>`.

## Key source files
- `lib/main.dart` — app entry (`main()`), `MyHomePage` / `_MyHomePageState`.
  Owns the first-page load flow: `_loadData()` -> `_getLocation()` ->
  `_getWeather()` -> `_updateWeather()`. Shows a `CircularProgressIndicator`
  until `loaded == true`.
- `lib/controller/weather_fetcher.dart` — `WeatherFetcher`: location
  lookup (`findLocation`), and the 3 OpenWeatherMap calls
  (`fetchNearestWeatherLocation`, `fetchHistoricalWeather`, `fetchWeather`).
- `lib/controller/services.dart` — `initializeService()` (background_fetch
  config + notification channels), `getServicePercentage()`, `getReportedFlightsNearMe()`.
- `lib/controller/nuptials.dart` — prediction math. `Nuptials` loads two
  RandomForest models; `nuptialDailyPercentageModel` / `nuptialHourlyPercentageModel`
  score weather via `lib/models/forest_model.dart` over the JSON assets.
- `lib/controller/arangodb.dart` — ArangoDB reporting of sightings/weather.
- `lib/view/map.dart` — map page. Weather overlays (clouds/wind/temp) are
  recoloured by `ColorFilter.matrix` to show nuptial-flight SUITABILITY:
  BLUE-GREEN/teal = favourable, RED = unfavourable, tinted per the model's PD
  distribution (see the map-shading note under Verification).
- `lib/responses/*.dart` — OWM response models.

## Models & feature wiring (IMPORTANT)
The two RandomForest models ship as **sklite JSON assets**
(`assets/final_model.json`, `assets/hour_model.json`) and are scored at runtime
by the hand-written pure-Dart tree-walker `lib/models/forest_model.dart`
(`ForestModel.fromJsonString(...)` -> `scorePositive(List<double>)`, which
computes sklearn-style `predict_proba` = mean of the per-tree normalised leaf
values). The old m2cgen-generated `lib/models/final_model.dart` /
`hour_model.dart` score trees were DELETED (2026-07-26) and `sklite` was
removed from `pubspec.yaml`. Do not hand-edit the JSON assets — regenerate
them from the training notebooks `lib/models/*.ipynb`.

`Nuptials.ensureLoaded()` parses both JSON assets once (via `rootBundle`);
it is kicked off after `runApp()` and awaited in the `_getWeather()` pipeline
before any scoring. Tests inject models with `Nuptials.loadFromStrings(...)`
(File IO, no rootBundle).

Each model expects features in a FIXED order that MUST match the training
notebook. The call sites in `lib/controller/nuptials.dart` must pass exactly:

- **Daily model** (`assets/final_model.json`, 21 inputs since 2026-07-26 part 4):
  `[lat, lon, hemisphere, sin_doy, cos_doy, temp(day), wind, rain(pop), humid,
  cloud, press, dewPoint, dew_dep, popNext1, popNext2, uvi, windGust, rainMm,
  daylength, moonSin, moonCos]`. The first 15 keep their old order; the 6 new
  fields are appended. NB `popNext1/popNext2` = FORECAST pop for the next two
  days (daily[i+1]/[i+2].pop), NOT antecedent rain (renamed from rain1/rain2).
- **Hourly model** (`assets/hour_model.json`, 14 inputs since 2026-07-26 part 4 —
  still NO rain, NO cloud, NO visibility): `[lat, lon, hemisphere, sin_doy,
  cos_doy, hour, temp, wind, humid, press, dewPoint, dew_dep, uvi, windGust]`
  (`hour` = UTC 0-23).

If a model is retrained with a different feature set/order, update BOTH the
call site in `nuptials.dart` AND the expectations in `test/nuptials_test.dart`,
`test/hourly_test.dart`, `test/model_test.dart`,
`test/production_model_parity_test.dart`.

The per-attribute "suitability" gauges (`temperatureContribution`,
`humidityContribution`, `windContribution`, `rainContribution`,
`cloudinessContribution`, `pressureContribution`, `uviContribution`) in
`nuptials.dart` are NOT hand-tuned distributions — they are derived from
the daily model (`assets/final_model.json` via `forest_model.dart`) via
per-feature partial-dependence curves (the model's
average flight probability as one feature varies, others held at
representative flight-season baseline contexts), normalised to [0,1].
Features the model barely uses (rain, pressure) resolve to a neutral 0.5.
`uviContribution` has no direct model input (UVI is not a feature in either
model) and delegates to the cloudiness curve via an inverse UVI→cloud map.

## First-load performance (work done 2026-07-18, commit `6123340`)
The first page was slow because the startup path blocked on serialised,
non-rendering work. Fixed:
1. `initializeService()` moved to *after* `runApp()` via `unawaited(...)`
   so background-fetch config no longer delays the first frame.
2. Notification-permission request in `_loadData()` is no longer `await`ed
   (wrapped in `unawaited(...)`) — location/weather calls start immediately.
3. `_getLocation()` now does a fast passive `getLastKnownPosition()` first
   and only falls back to an active GPS fix when none exists, avoiding the
   previous double full weather fetch on every launch. Also fixes the
   first-launch case (no cached position) which used to throw.
4. Active-GPS `timeLimit` reduced 30s -> 10s (`weather_fetcher.dart`).

Response caching is implemented in `weather_fetcher.dart` via `_fetchCached`
(`shared_preferences`, keyed by rounded lat/lon). Per-endpoint TTLs: 30 min
current/forecast, 24 h reverse geocode, 30 days historical. Repeat launches
reuse cached responses, cutting paid OWM calls.

